# `distr::Exponential`

The exponential distribution: the waiting time between independent events that occur at a constant rate.

## Signature

```cpp
pluss::Series distr::pdf(dst::Exponential, const pluss::Series& x, const std::vector<double>& params);
pluss::Series distr::logpdf(dst::Exponential, const pluss::Series& x, const std::vector<double>& params);
pluss::Series distr::cdf(dst::Exponential, const pluss::Series& x, const std::vector<double>& params);
pluss::Series distr::logcdf(dst::Exponential, const pluss::Series& x, const std::vector<double>& params);
pluss::Series distr::sample(dst::Exponential, const std::vector<double>& params, std::initializer_list<std::int64_t> sizes, pluss::Config cfg = {}, std::uint64_t seed = 0);
```

## What It Does

The exponential distribution is the waiting time until the next event of a Poisson process, and the only continuous distribution without memory: given that you have waited `t` already, the remaining wait has the same distribution as at the start. It has a single parameter, the `rate`, with mean `1/rate` and variance `1/rate²`, and its density is largest at zero and falls off exponentially.

The parameters, in the order of the `params` list:

| Parameter | Constraint | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `rate` | > 0 | the number of events per unit time; the mean waiting time is `1/rate` |

Support: `x >= 0`.

The object form of this family, with the same functions as methods, is on its own page: [`distr::Exponential`](../object_based/Exponential.md).

## Parameters

`const pluss::Series& x`
: The points at which to evaluate, a `Series` of any shape; the result has the same shape. Points outside the support have density zero and distribution value 0 or 1.

`const std::vector<double>& params`
: The parameters of the family, in the order shown in the table above. A list of the wrong length is refused.

`std::initializer_list<std::int64_t> sizes`
: The shape of the array of random draws, for example `{n}` or `{rows, columns}`.

`pluss::Config cfg`
: The dtype and platform of the draws; the default is Float32 on the CPU, so request Float64 when precision matters.

`std::uint64_t seed`
: The seed of the stream. The same seed always gives the same draws; a seed of 0 is a fixed default, not a random one.

## Returns

`pdf`, `logpdf`, `cdf` and `logcdf` return a `Series` of the shape of `x`; `sample` returns a `Series` of the requested shape.

## Errors

- Parameters outside their valid range are refused with an error that names the requirement (`rate` must be positive).
- A `params` list of the wrong length is refused.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

Example 1 evaluates the density (mass) function, the distribution function and the log-density of the Exponential on a small grid with `distr::pdf`, `distr::cdf` and `distr::logpdf`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
namespace dst = distr::dst;

int main() {
  const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
  std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(5);

  const auto x = pluss::make_series({0.0, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 4.0, 8.0}, f64);
  const std::vector<double> params = {0.7};

  std::cout << "x:";
  for (double v : x.pull<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "pdf:";
  for (double v : distr::pdf(dst::Exponential, x, params).pull<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "cdf:";
  for (double v : distr::cdf(dst::Exponential, x, params).pull<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "logpdf:";
  for (double v : distr::logpdf(dst::Exponential, x, params).pull<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
}
```

The density is largest on this grid at x = 0, where it is 0.7; the distribution function runs from 0 at x = 0 to 0.9963 at x = 8, and it is the running total of the density. `logpdf` is the logarithm of the density, for example log(0.7) = -0.35667 at x = 0.

### Example 2

Example 2 checks that the three descriptions of the same probability agree: the integral of the density (sum of the masses) over a range, the difference of the distribution function at its ends, and the fraction of random draws that land in it.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
namespace dst = distr::dst;

int main() {
  const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
  std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(5);
  const std::vector<double> params = {0.7};

  // The three ways to the probability that the variable lies in [0.5, 3]:
  const auto grid = pluss::series::linspace(0.5, 3.0, 3001, f64);
  const double area = as_f64(distr::pdf(dst::Exponential, grid, params).antidiff(grid).trapezoid());

  const auto edges = pluss::make_series({0.5, 3.0}, f64);
  const auto c = distr::cdf(dst::Exponential, edges, params).pull<double>();

  const auto draws = distr::sample(dst::Exponential, params, {200000}, f64, 7ULL);
  const double fraction = as_f64(((draws >= 0.5).to(pluss::ScalarType::Float64) * (draws <= 3.0).to(pluss::ScalarType::Float64)).mean());

  std::cout << "integral of the density: " << area << '\n';
  std::cout << "cdf(3) - cdf(0.5): " << c[1] - c[0] << '\n';
  std::cout << "fraction of 200000 draws in range: " << fraction << '\n';
}
```

The integral of the density (0.58223) and the difference of the distribution function (0.58223) agree to all printed digits: they are two computations of one probability. The fraction of random draws (0.58018) differs from it by 0.00205, which is the size of the sampling error of a fraction of 200000 draws (a standard error of about 0.0011), so the density, the distribution function and the sampler describe the same distribution.

### Example 3

Example 3 shows how the shape changes with the parameters: the density at the same points for 3 parameter settings.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
namespace dst = distr::dst;

int main() {
  const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
  std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(4);
  const auto x = pluss::make_series({0.0, 0.25, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 4.0}, f64);

  std::cout << "x:";
  for (double v : x.pull<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "rate 0.3:";
  for (double v : distr::pdf(dst::Exponential, x, {0.3}).pull<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "rate 1  :";
  for (double v : distr::pdf(dst::Exponential, x, {1.0}).pull<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "rate 3  :";
  for (double v : distr::pdf(dst::Exponential, x, {3.0}).pull<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
}
```

Read down the rows: `rate 0.3` peaks on this grid at x = 0 (value 0.3); `rate 1` peaks on this grid at x = 0 (value 1); `rate 3` peaks on this grid at x = 0 (value 3). The parameters move the location, the width and the shape of the curve, which is what choosing a member of the family means.

### Example 4

Example 4 checks the distribution function against its closed form, `1 - exp(-rate x)`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
namespace dst = distr::dst;

int main() {
  const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
  std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(6);
  const auto x = pluss::make_series({0.0, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 4.0, 8.0}, f64);
  const std::vector<double> params = {0.7};

  const auto formula = 1.0 - (x * -0.7).exp();
  const auto library = distr::cdf(dst::Exponential, x, params);

  std::cout << "x:";
  for (double v : x.pull<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "formula:";
  for (double v : formula.pull<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "distr::cdf:";
  for (double v : library.pull<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << std::scientific << std::setprecision(2) << "largest difference: " << as_f64((formula - library).abs().max()) << '\n';
}
```

The formula and `distr::cdf` agree to 0 over the grid, so the library implements exactly the closed form quoted above; the same expression is the quickest way to check a parameterisation before relying on it.

### Example 5

Example 5 confirms that an exponential distribution with rate 0.5 is a gamma distribution with shape 1 and scale 2.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
namespace dst = distr::dst;

int main() {
  const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
  std::cout << std::scientific << std::setprecision(2);
  const auto x = pluss::make_series({0.1, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0}, f64);
  const std::vector<double> here = {0.5};
  const std::vector<double> there = {0.0, 2.0, 1.0};

  const double dpdf = as_f64((distr::pdf(dst::Exponential, x, here) - distr::pdf(dst::Gamma, x, there)).abs().max());
  const double dcdf = as_f64((distr::cdf(dst::Exponential, x, here) - distr::cdf(dst::Gamma, x, there)).abs().max());
  std::cout << "largest difference in the density: " << dpdf << '\n';
  std::cout << "largest difference in the distribution function: " << dcdf << '\n';
}
```

The two largest differences are 6.94e-18 for the density and 9.99e-16 for the distribution function over the grid: zero up to rounding, so the two descriptions are the same distribution.

